\f1\fs22 Chilo found Johnny sulking over a gold-leaf cappuccino that cost more than a waiter\'97usually an emaciated wraith hoping to break into modelling\'97made in the caf\'e9 in a month. The price of the coffee made a certain amount of economic sense; it was brewed from beans shat from the arses of fashion models trying to make a little extra. It was easy money; the beans filled their bellies, and yielded almost no calories as long as they passed through fast enough. There were methods for making that happen. The waiter could\'92ve been \'91packing\'92 while serving Johnny. Johnny hadn\'92t touched the coffee in over fifteen minutes; it had turned tepid and looked like silty water in the birdbath of a cup. He was in a state of torpor, the kind born of depression and the fear of any movement making matters worse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>None of these facts about Johnny\'92s coffee were immediately available to Chilo, who was standing outside and wrapping on the window, trying to get Johnny\'92s attention. The caf\'e9 admitted only Airborne clientele; Chilo was mildy satisfied he was allowed on this high street promenade so high above the Layer. To Chilo, Johnny appeared catatonic, so he wrapped harder on the window which, of course, unsettled the clientele.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Excuse me,\'92 the waiter said to Johnny. \'91There appears to be a deranged old man outside, trying to get your attention.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Johnny asked, then looked at the waiter, then processed what they\'92d said. He blinked, processed a little more, and summoned enough energy for the effort of turning his head and looking towards the window. \'91Send him over,\'92 he said flatly. He didn\'92t care who he associated with any more, nor who saw who he was associating with.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sir?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You heard me. Send him over,\'92 Johnny said without raising his voice, so perhaps the waiter hadn\'92t heard him. Perhaps they\'92d divined what Johnny wanted, because their shoulders slumped and they gave a queasy look as if Johnny had ordered them to pat a rat. Resigned to acquiescing to Johnny\'92s station in life, the waiter made busy to usher Chilo to the table, so they could be done with such unpleasantness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91May sit?\'92 Chilo asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Who are you?\'92 Johnny asked, without a trace of verve.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo frowned, then with the showmanship of a magician, extracted a card from a pocket, and presented it to Johnny. Johnny failed to take it, so Chilo placed it on the table for Johnny to read.\
