<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You means it sucks like nothing else.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And there Chilo realised the error of crude analogies, but he was committed now. \'91It would be more accurate to say it both sucks and blows.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But a black hole sucks everything in its wake.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A popular misconception, young Stoat. Black holes also 
\f2\i emit
\f1\i0  radiation. You see, powerful quantum vacuum fluctuations convert into pairs of particles, one of which escapes the event horizon at the speed of infinity, while \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny regarded Chilo with impatient superiority. \'91You\'92re talking about Hawking radiation. Please don\'92t presume to talk down to me,\'92 he said, talking down to Chilo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Apologies.\'92 Chilo slurped at his coffee again while he reappraised his tactics. \'91Look, in the case of a Gyges disc, the accretion of energy is both more intense and more local to a four-dimensional physical space, and is, at least theoretically, limitless.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There is no such thing as limitless,\'92 Johnny said, feeling he was getting the better of this tussle of minds.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo shrugged. \'91Think of a Gyges disc as inevitably unstable. Spin it fast enough or for long enough, and the phenomena you experienced is guaranteed to occur.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny soured. \'91So the whole research project was doomed from the start.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo held his coffee cup in mid air and meditated upon it. He knew he could back out. Perhaps there\'92d be other opportunities, if only Kleoboulos wasn\'92t so sure the prophecy required certain events occur by Christmas. \'91Not necessarily.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hope cast its sunny aspect upon Johnny\'92s gloom. \'91What?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The reaction can be stabilised. You simply need something to suck everything the Gyges disc blows.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It sounded to Johnny like all the times he\'92d haggled with a sex worker. That never turned out well either. Incredulous, he blurted, \'91Where I am supposed to find a sucker like that?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You need a binary system,\'92 Chilo said somewhat enigmatically, and waited to see if Johnny could follow the drift. He couldn\'92t, so Chilo pressed on. \'91If you precisely position another Gyges disc in relation to the first, and spin it at the same speed, it will suck what the first is blowing, while the first will suck what the second is blowing.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So kinda like some cosmic sixty-nine event.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Quite,\'92 Chilo said, and blinked slowly and deliberately in the manner that was said to make friends with cats. He felt a wry smile on his face and wondered how it got there. \'91Think of it more like the shape of the symbol for infinity. Achieve that shape, energetically, between a binary relationship of discs, and you will achieve \'85\'92 Chilo reconsidered his words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A stable energetic state?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, suffice to say, we\'92ll all get what we richly deserve \'85 you included, of course.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny had always dreamed of getting what he deserved, especially richly. And he was only \'85 \'91Where am I going to find another Gyges disc?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo paused, then capitulated to his destiny. He reached into his pocket, withdrew something slim and dark. Johnny\'92s mouth fell agape when the leathery, scaly object flared metallic-shiny for a brief second. Chilo pushed it across the smooth linen to Johnny. \'91Here. Have mine.\'92
